  "updated": "2026-09-04T11:21:54.470361+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Heat and humidity continues Friday. Break in the\nheat late in the weekend, but heat builds again towards the \nmiddle of next week.\n\nThe influence of the upper level ridge that has remained over\nthe region looks to linger for two more days this week before an\nupper level trough starts to retrograde westward into the Mid-\nAtlantic states by Saturday and Sunday. High temperatures \nwill reach the mid to upper 90s in the Piedmont today, potentially \nreaching 100 degrees for some locations, which is 10 to 17 degrees \nabove seasonal normals. Heat indices will range from  100 to 105 \ntoday, though any cloud cover and precipitation could prevent \nreaching the upper end of that range. With surface dewpoints\nmixing out on Thursday into the low to mid 60s across the\nPiedmont, many areas never saw heat index values reach Heat\nAdvisory criteria; therefore, a Heat Advisory will not be issued\nfor today across the area as dewpoints will likely do the same\nthing today. This mixing out of surface dewpoints combined with\nshower and thunderstorm chances will likely reduce chances for\nheat index values to reach the 105 mark across the piedmonts of\nVA and NC in our area today. Temperatures look to remain hot\nagain on Saturday, but slightly cooler, with highs in the low \nto mid 90s across the Piedmont, and low to mid 80s across the \nmountains. \n\nThe aforementioned retrograding trough will bring much lower \n500mb heights and drier air to the region late on Saturday and\ninto Sunday. These lower 500mb heights will subsequently reduce\ntemperatures to near normal values for this time of the year, \nwith highs in the low to mid 80s east of the Blue Ridge, and \nupper 70s to low 80s west of the Blue Ridge. \n\nModel guidance is trending towards the upper level ridge\nbuilding back east into the Mid-Atlantic states and the\nSoutheast by the middle of next week. This looks to lead to hot\nweather possibly returning to the region; however, headline\nvalues of heat don't look likely at this time. \n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Strong to severe thunderstorms possible Friday \nand Saturday.\n\nA westward retrograding upper level trough over the northeast\nwill push back the upper level ridge that has maintained itself\nover the region over the last couple of days. As this occurs,\nthe amount of subsidence across the area should decrease as the\nupper level ridge retreats west. This will allow storms to also\ntrack through the region from the northwest from pieces of\nshortwave energy track southeast along the eastern periphery of\nthe aforementioned upper level ridge. Storm activity today \nlooks to remain fairly scattered in nature, as the influence of \nthe upper level trough remains well to the northeast; however, \nthe environment supports strong damaging wind gusts across the \nregion with 1500-2000 J/Kg of SBCAPE, and 1000-1400 J/Kg of \nDCAPE. Any storms that develop in this environment will likely \nproduce strong damaging outflows through the late afternoon and \nevening hours. \n\nBy Saturday, the upper level trough looks to retreat further\nsouth and west into the Mid Atlantic states. The timing of \nanother shortwave trough rounding the upper level ridge and \nejecting southeastward out of the Great Lakes region will\ndetermine the threat for severe weather across the region. If it\noccurs too early in the morning on Saturday, the threat for\nsevere thunderstorms in the afternoon and evening will likely be\ndiminished; however, if a disturbance pushes through the area\nduring the afternoon and evening, then strong to severe storms\nwill be likely areawide. At this time, CAM guidance has a\nsimilar environment developing Saturday afternoon, with SBCAPE\nvalues in the 1500-2000 J/Kg range, and DCAPE values in the\n1000-1400 J/Kg range. Any storms developing in this type of\nenvironment will once again be capable of producing strong to\ndamaging wind gusts in outflows.",
